  * front of the transmit shift register, a two-character receive buffer,
  * and the INTFLAG rules: DRE is cleared only by writing DATA, TXC by W1C or
  * by writing DATA, RXC only by reading DATA or disabling the receiver.
+ *
+ * The SPI host (MODE 3) shifts each DATA write out on the instance's SSI
+ * bus at once and takes the reply into a two-character receive buffer
+ * (CTRLB.RXEN). With DIPO on the pad DOPO drives, the input is the output
+ * (a loopback through the pad); with no device on the bus the line reads
+ * 0xFF. A character arriving with the buffer full is lost; when the buffer
+ * runs empty STATUS.BUFOVF and INTFLAG.ERROR are set and a 0 takes the lost
+ * character's place (PIC32CM MC00 silicon).
+ *
+ * The I2C host (MODE 5) runs transfers on the instance's I2C bus: ADDR
+ * starts one (write: MB; read: the first byte and SB, or MB on a NACK),
+ * DATA writes send a byte (MB, STATUS.RXNACK), DATA reads in smart mode
+ * acknowledge and fetch the next byte, CTRLB.CMD gives repeated start,
+ * byte read and stop. STATUS.BUSSTATE is UNKNOWN after enable until
+ * software forces IDLE, OWNER during a transfer and for a moment after
+ * the stop (silicon). Transfers complete at once; bus timing, arbitration,
+ * timeouts, 10-bit and high-speed addressing are not modelled.
+ *
  * Register semantics (W1C, set/clear aliases, enable protection, views,
  * SWRST) come from the generated tables through mchp-regblock.
  *
@@ -41,6 +59,21 @@
 
 #define MCHP_SERCOM_RX_DEPTH    2
 
+#define MCHP_SERCOM_MODE_SPIM   3
+#define MCHP_SERCOM_MODE_I2CM   5
